    "note": "candidate-007 — fixed the multiple-0-values bug you found on candidate-006. Root cause: the smooth (Catmull-Rom) interpolation OVERSHOOTS past the data — a sharp drop to the axis floor (your run of 12 zeros after Aug=36) made the spline dip BELOW the baseline, reading as fake-negative data under the axis. Fix: clamp the flattened polyline to the plot's vertical bounds [plotY, baselineY], so neither under- nor over-shoot escapes the axes. Now the line drops to 0 and rides flat along the baseline (no sub-axis dip); 0-value markers sit on the axis as expected. Verified: trailing-0s, all-0s, mid-0s, negatives all valid + clean; default/linear/stepped unchanged (clamp is a no-op for in-range data).\n\nPROCESS NOTE (acting on your feedback): I froze candidate-006_humanedits.mosaic BEFORE editing the template, so it captures the actual broken 0-value render. I also hardened agent.md: the new hard rule is FREEZE _humanedits.mosaic FIRST on any negative feedback / prop diff, BEFORE touching the template — because the template changes between candidates and a post-fix flatten can't reproduce the bug. (Honest debt: candidate-003 & 004's broken renders are unrecoverable — I flattened them after their fixes shipped. Their breaking PROPS survive in the live .mosaicx, but the broken pixels are gone. Won't happen again.)\n\nAlso filed the desktop drift + GeometricPreview fidelity as .ai/proposed-plans/desktop-cli-render-parity.md.",
